PHP2SHELL: AUDIT OF THIS TREE'S PHP RUNTIMES

FreeMED 0.9.x - PHP interpretation-layer RCE, CVE-2026-109618 / CVE-2026-109619
Audited 2026-10-23 on branch security/mitigation-2026-09 at d4484882.

This file is an INVENTORY AND EXPOSURE AUDIT. It changes no code. Every number
in it was measured on this host on 2026-10-23 unless the row says "cited"; the
command that produced it is next to it. Where something could not be measured
it says so in section 8 instead of being inferred.


1. WHAT THE VULNERABILITY IS, AND WHY APPLICATION CODE CANNOT MITIGATE IT

Two critical flaws in PHP's request-processing layer, published 2026-10-20/21:

  CVE-2026-109618  RCE after a memory allocation failure interrupts request
                   initialisation (form/multipart parsing); PHP's cleanup then
                   treats the half-built hash bucket as a valid value.
  CVE-2026-109619  RCE via stale request-owned global state reused by a later
                   request on the same worker (fatal-error backtraces on 8.5;
                   session upload processing on mod_php AND php-fpm).

Both are unauthenticated RCE with the privileges of the web-server account,
CVSS v4.0 9.2 each. Fixed releases:

  8.2 -> 8.2.35 | 8.3 -> 8.3.36 | 8.4 -> 8.4.27 | 8.5 -> 8.5.12
  (cited: PHP release announcement; CERT-EU advisory 2026-018; NCSC-NL
   php2shell repository, README, status 2026-10-23.)

Affected SAPIs, cited from the PHP announcement: mod_php, PHP-FPM, php-cgi,
FrankenPHP, LiteSpeed. PHP rates mod_php as the highest risk because the
request is parsed inside the server process before application code runs.
PHP 8.1 and older are out of support; the PHP project published source
backports for 7.4, 8.0 and 8.1 only. PHP 5.x has no upstream fix.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS TREE: no edit to any .php file in this repository can
close either CVE. The payload is consumed by libphp/php-fpm before index.php,
relay.php or lib/freemed.php is entered, so sessions, ACLs, the relay
allowlist, the SQL gates and every other control this branch built are all
downstream of the vulnerable code. The only remediation is the interpreter
version (plus, as a stopgap, the mitigations in section 6). Anyone reading a
FreeMED security review should see that stated, because SECURITY.md and
doc/SECURITY_ADVISORY otherwise read as though the application is the whole
attack surface.

Temporary mitigations, cited (PHP guidance, restated by CERT-EU and Datadog).
Neither is a fix:
  109618: memory_limit=-1 plus decoded-body size limits at the front end and
          memory limits on the container; do NOT use RLIMIT_AS/RLIMIT_DATA.
  109619: session.upload_progress.enabled=0 and, on PHP 8.5,
          fatal_error_backtraces=0; the settings must be in configuration that
          loads before the request is parsed, and every worker must be
          restarted afterwards.


