3.1 THIS TREE'S INI FILE IS NOT READ BY THE SHIPPED CONTAINER

docker/docker-compose.yml mounts:

  ./php-custom.ini:/opt/bitnami/php/etc/conf.d/custom.ini:ro

/opt/bitnami does not exist in that image (`ls /opt/bitnami` -> "No such file
or directory"); the image is a stock docker-library PHP 8.3-fpm whose ini
directory is /usr/local/etc/php/conf.d. Docker creates the missing mountpoint
and mounts the file there, and PHP never scans it. Ablation - run the shipped
image with the compose mount in place and read the values back:

  docker run --rm \
    -v "$PWD/docker/php-custom.ini:/opt/bitnami/php/etc/conf.d/custom.ini:ro" \
    --entrypoint php jbuchbinder/moodle-php-8.3-fpm -i \
    | grep -E '^(memory_limit|post_max_size|max_file_uploads|register_argc_argv|session.upload_progress.enabled)'

  memory_limit                  128M     (php-custom.ini says 2048M)
  post_max_size                 8M
  max_file_uploads              20       (php-custom.ini says 128M)
  register_argc_argv            On       (php-custom.ini says Off)
  session.upload_progress.enabled On

i.e. the mount changes nothing. Two consequences, both of which matter here:

  * CORRECTION to doc/DEPLOYMENT_HARDENING:211-225. That block says the drop-in
    "predates this change and carries settings the deployment has been using
    (memory_limit, max_input_vars), so the mechanism is in use". The image is
    now inspectable: the repo's php-custom.ini is not read by it, and the
    deployment is running PHP's compiled defaults (plus max_input_vars=10000
    from a custom.ini baked into the image itself). The register_argc_argv = Off
    line this branch shipped has therefore never taken effect in this layout.
    The behaviour the branch fixed (vcalendar's SAPI test) is unaffected, but the
    ini claim needs the same treatment: measured, not assumed.
  * Any PHP2Shell mitigation written into docker/php-custom.ini today would be
    inert for exactly the same reason. Fixing the mount path is a prerequisite
    for mitigation 109619, not a tidy-up.

3.2 NO REQUEST-SIZE CONTROL AT EITHER EDGE

  docker/nginx.conf      client_max_body_size 10000M          (10 GB)
  doc/freemed.apache.conf  no LimitRequestBody directive anywhere in the tree
                           (`grep -rn LimitRequestBody .` -> no hits)

PHP's own post_max_size=8M is the only cap on a decoded body, and it is a
compiled default that the repo's own ini (if it were ever loaded) does not set
- while the same file raises max_file_uploads to 128M. 109618's documented
mitigation is a decoded-body limit at the front end; there is none.


4. EXPOSURE OF THE TWO CVEs IN THESE LAYOUTS

CVE-2026-109619 (stale worker state) - LIVE in both web layouts:
  * session.upload_progress.enabled is On (PHP default, and the shipped ini does
    not touch it). PHP enables upload-progress tracking by default and it runs
    even when the application never calls session_start(); this application
    does call it in lib/freemed.php on every request and has four upload
    modules (UnfiledDocuments, PhotographicIdentification, DicomModule,
    ScannedDocuments) that consume $_FILES.
  * The same-worker requirement is eased by the pool size: pm.max_children = 5.
    A small pool raises the per-attempt success rate (cited: Datadog writeup).
  * 8.3.31 is affected; the 8.5-only fatal_error_backtraces half does not apply
    to this branch, but the upload half is confirmed by PHP to give command
    execution on FPM as well as mod_php.

CVE-2026-109618 (memory-exhaustion during parse) - reachable in both web
layouts, with an accidental and undocumented bound:
  * Nothing in PHP or the edges refuses a hostile multipart body: nginx allows
    10 GB, Apache's layout sets no LimitRequestBody, and no rate or request-size
    control exists in this tree.
  * The effective cap is post_max_size=8M with max_input_vars=10000 and
    memory_limit=128M - compiled defaults, not controls. An operator who follows
    the repo's own ini (memory_limit=2048M, max_file_uploads=128M) or who raises
    post_max_size for document uploads moves the parse window. Treat the current
    posture as "not deliberately bounded", not as "mitigated".
  * The Debian layout is the worse of the two for this CVE: PHP rates mod_php
    highest risk because the parse happens in the server process, and php5 is
    unpatchable.

For both: application authentication is irrelevant. Nothing before section 6 of
this document changes exploitability by a single request.


5. VERDICT PER LAYOUT

  1. docker/docker-compose.yml (shipped container)  WAS VULNERABLE - PHP 8.3.31.
     Fix = rebuild on >= 8.3.36; five patch releases behind, referenced as
     :latest with no digest. FIXED: section 10 (a), image built from the new
     docker/Dockerfile at 8.3.36.
  2. debian/ (package)  WAS VULNERABLE AND WITHOUT A PATCH PATH - php5 + mod_php.
     The dependency line had to move to a supported interpreter.
     FIXED: section 10 (e), dependency line now php8.3 / libapache2-mod-php8.3.
  3. doc/freemed.apache.conf (reference vhost)  STILL UNDETERMINED - it names no
     PHP version. Any site using it with a distro PHP is vulnerable unless the
     distro package carries the backport; distro backports often do not change
     the upstream version string, so check the distro advisory, not `php -v`.
     Its body cap and the two ini lines the operator must carry are now in the
     file (section 10 (d)).
  4. Harness images / local php:8.3-cli cache  WAS STALE - 8.3.35, and nothing
     in this tree would notice. FIXED: section 10 (f), rebuilt at 8.3.36 with a
     build-time assertion and a runtime gate in `make security-check`.
  5. Application code (this branch's security work)  NOT THE FIX and not at
     fault: no application-level change can close either CVE.


6. REMEDIATION, IN ORDER

  a. Rebuild the shipped image on php:8.3.36 or later 8.3.x, publish a new tag,
     and pin it in docker/docker-compose.yml by digest (a floating :latest is
     how a 2026-05-27 build stayed in service through 2026-10-21).
  b. Correct the ini mount to a path the image scans:
     ./php-custom.ini:/usr/local/etc/php/conf.d/zz-freemed.ini:ro
     Then verify from INSIDE the container (`php --ini`, `php -i`); a mount at
     the wrong path fails silently, which is what section 3.1 measures.
  c. Add to that ini, before relying on it:
       session.upload_progress.enabled = Off
       fatal_error_backtraces = 0        ; PHP 8.5 only; harmless elsewhere
     and restart every worker - the settings are read at process start.
     Where the site runs its own php.ini (Debian, Apache layout), the same two
     lines go there; docker/php-custom.ini covers the container only.
  d. Put a real body cap at each edge: nginx client_max_body_size (10 GB today)
     with per-location exceptions for the upload endpoints, and LimitRequestBody
     in doc/freemed.apache.conf.
  e. Move debian/control off php5 (php8.3-* packages / libapache2-mod-php8.3),
     or state in README.md and the package description that the Debian path is
     unsupported.
  f. Rebuild freemed-verify-php:apache and :fpm, and add a version assertion to
     the guard scripts so a measurement taken on a vulnerable interpreter fails
     loudly. tests/security/evidence headers should record `php -v`.
  g. Operationally: after the image swap, restart/replace workers - an old fpm
     worker keeps the old binary resident.


